A||||4||Leadfoot||rear break line problems||||1||
Z||000000||Leadfoot||07-03-2001||06:28 PM||||Recently the main brake line which feeds into the distribution block (located at the right tire well) sprung a leak.  I searched all the local shops like pep boys, napa and super track and none of them have the large metric, bubble flanged tubes.  I did a forum search and found quite a few people had the same problem.  In one post a guy used a compression ring to join to the the tubes.  I realize this is illegal but what other type of fitting can be used?  I thought about using 1/4 in. s.s. swaglock union fitting.  When I told the guy at Napa my problem he gave me a steel union and said the as long as the connection is not brass/copper it's legal.?? Anyone see a problem with this?||||reg||1
Z||000001||PBJ||07-03-2001||06:45 PM||||you could get/rent a buble flare tool and make the line youself.  I don't have any experience with the other union's you were asking about.  All GM shops MUST have a buble flare tool for the two sizes and could help you make one.||||reg||1
Z||000002||SteveJ||07-03-2001||07:30 PM||||Autozone sold me 1/4 tubing and a fitting that went into the "Y" fitting at the rear wheel and another that went into the prop valve. Problem solved.||||reg||1
Z||000003||isthiswhereiputausername?||07-03-2001||09:58 PM||||I just started reinstalling the lines on my car today..<P>I rented a flaring tool & a pipe bender <BR>(pipe bender is not really needed if your careful) <P>I am replacing mine from master cylinder to all 4 wheels.<P><BR>I found I had a hard time also finding the fittings So I decided to reuse the old ones.<P>If your old ones are rusty and the pipe won't come free from the brakeline, the brakeline can be drilled out from the fitting. <BR>MAKE SURE ITS CLEAN WHEN YOU PUT IT BACK ON!<P>Another thing I did with mine, I wrapped my tubing ends with black tape. It prevents any dirt or moisture from entering the pipes when i'm fitting it into place and easy enough to pull off when its ready to attach.<P>Also, If the place you went to can't rent a flaring tool, fit the pipe in place then take it back in and get them to flare it if they will(the shop SHOULD have one there)<P><BR>Good luck!! <P><p>[This message has been edited by isthiswhereiputausername? (edited 07-03-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000004||theogre||07-04-2001||12:04 AM||||You'd have to check local laws, but as far as I know,,, steel body compression fitings are ok. (The seal portion is still brass.)Brass/copper bodies can crack after repeated cycles. Keep in mind, you're dealing with as much as 1000PSI or more. Everyting in the system has to be made to deal with that. Most Common brass/copper plumbing parts can't take it. they may last years or minutes. no way to predict.||||reg||1
